            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Mortal danger, the transition of physical death, or profound suffering and despair. In Christian teaching, it most frequently symbolizes the literal end of life or a period of intense, life-threatening trial through which God's presence provides comfort.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Psalm 23:4\",\"Job 10:21-22\",\"Jeremiah 2:6\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Spiritual darkness, ignorance, or the state of humanity lost in sin and despair before the arrival of Christ.\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 9:2\",\"Matthew 4:16\",\"Luke 1:79\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Deep darkness or pitch blackness, understood simply as an intense physical or atmospheric darkness (reflecting modern linguistic consensus on the Hebrew word tsalmaveth rather than a literal shadow of death).\",\"basis\":[\"Job 3:5\",\"Job 34:22\",\"Amos 5:8\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While popular piety heavily associates the phrase with physical dying due to the use of Psalm 23 at funerals, academic scholarship often translates the underlying Hebrew as 'deep darkness.' Furthermore, prophetic and New Testament literature frequently use it to symbolize spiritual oppression and hopelessness relieved by the Messiah.\"}",
